<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/16th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">War of the Landshut Succession</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1503">1503</a>–<a href="/years/1505">1505</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The War of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landshut">Landshut</a> Succession was fought in 1503–1505 over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria-landshut">Duchy of Bavaria-Landshut</a>. Duke George the Rich had left his lands to his son-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rupert-of-germany">Rupert of the Palatinate</a> in breach of Wittelsbach family agreements, and Albert IV of Bavaria-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> went to war. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a> arbitration of 1505 gave most of the lands to Albert, reuniting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palatinate-neuburg">Palatinate-Neuburg</a> was created for Rupert's sons.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under the Wittelsbachs the duchy's wars were often internal: partitions led to fraternal conflicts, and the <b>Landshut War of Succession</b> of 1503–1505 ended only through imperial arbitration.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-bavaria">Duchy of Bavaria · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Landshut War of Succession</b> (1503–1505), fought over the claims of Elector Philip's son Rupert to the Bavarian inheritance, ended in defeat, the loss of lands to Bavaria and Württemberg and the creation of the smaller Palatinate-Neuburg for Rupert's sons.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electoral-palatinate">Electoral Palatinate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>War of the Landshut Succession</b> of 1503 to 1505 gave most lands to Albert IV of Bavaria-Munich, while Palatinate-Neuburg was created for Rupert's sons.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-bavaria-landshut">Duchy of Bavaria-Landshut</a></li><li><blockquote>Albert IV won the <b>War of the Landshut Succession</b> in 1505 and reunited most of Bavaria.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-bavaria-munich">Duchy of Bavaria-Munich</a></li><li><blockquote>Nuremberg turned Lutheran in 1525 and won a large rural territory in the <b>Landshut War</b> of 1504 to 1505.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-nuremberg">Free Imperial City of Nuremberg</a></li><li><blockquote>The Cologne arbitration of 1505 that ended the <b>War of the Landshut Succession</b> created a new principality from parts of the Duchy of Bavaria-Landshut for Otto Henry and Philip, the young sons of Rupert of the Palatinate.</blockquote><a href="/state/palatinate-neuburg">Palatinate-Neuburg</a></li></ul></section>
